{"attack":true,"case":"The target conflates checklist compliance with a reliability claim while never addressing the problem's required distinguishing evidence.\nThe problem demands identification of what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. The target's claim, mechanism ('Heuristic Depth Mechanism'), and counterconditions never specify any within-evaluation evidential marker that would separate genuine reliability from coincidental compliance; instead it asserts the expert is more reliable and that non-experts 'fail catastrophically,' which is the very reliability claim needing evidential separation.\ncites: 5e979b20f67e5b5e43141755fa9b3c23afdbc57239db9e7799e2e0dc95d58e71","counterexample":[{"case":"A single evaluation where non-experts score higher than the expert on verifiable outcomes yet show no evidence of independent judgment: every item they got right was an explicit checklist step, every item they got wrong was an unlisted edge case, and their explanations merely restate checklist lines.","distinguishing_evidence_absent_in_target":"The target provides no criterion, observable, or comparison that would let an evaluator tell whether the non-experts' higher score reflects genuine reliability or mere compliance tracking the answer key.","why_this_breaks_the_target":"Under the target's own 'false ceiling' story, non-experts can achieve high accuracy on common cases and still lack genuine reliability; without the required evidence marker, the target cannot distinguish this from a real reliability gain, so its claim that the expert 'will remain more reliable' is unsupported by the problem's standard."}]}